/-!
# Graded algebras on an algebraic space, and the data of the relative Proj

The relative homogeneous spectrum `Proj_X(𝒜)` of an algebraic space `X` over a scheme `S` is
built from a quasi-coherent graded `𝒪_X`-algebra `𝒜`.  Stacks 0849 fixes that data once and
for all, and the whole section on relative Proj is stated in it.  This file records it.

The basic properties of the structure morphism `π : Proj_X(𝒜) ⟶ X` are then read off an étale
scheme chart of `X`: by Stacks 084B the base change of `π` along a scheme `Z ⟶ X` is the
relative Proj of the pulled-back algebra on `Z`, and quasi-compactness and finite type are
étale local on the target, so each follows from the case of schemes.  This file records that
chart presentation and the two properties it yields, Stacks 084F and 084G.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.RelativeProjSituation` is the data of Stacks 0849: a scheme `S`, an
  algebraic space `X` over `S`, and a quasi-coherent graded `𝒪_X`-algebra.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.RelativeProjChart` presents a morphism of algebraic spaces as a
  relative Proj on a surjective étale scheme chart of its target: the chart, a relative Proj
  situation of Stacks 01NN on it, and a cartesian square identifying the base change of the
  morphism with the scheme-level relative Proj of Stacks 01NQ.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.relativeProjChartOfSituation` inhabits that structure for every
  relative Proj situation of Stacks 01NN contained in the site, on the trivial chart of its
  base.  It is the non-vacuity witness for the two main results.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.RelativeProjPoints` presents a morphism of algebraic spaces as a
  relative Proj over *all* the scheme points of its target: for every object `T` of the site
  and every `T`-point `f` of the target, a quasi-coherent graded algebra on the scheme of `T`
  — the restriction `f^*𝒜` — and a cartesian square identifying `Proj_T(f^*𝒜)` with `T ×_X P`.
  This is the second assertion of Stacks 084B, and it is what the proof of Stacks 085D uses.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isSeparated_relativeProj` is **Stacks 084E**: the structure
  morphism of the relative Proj is separated, with no hypothesis on the graded algebra.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isQuasiCompact_relativeProj` is **Stacks 084F**: the structure
  morphism of the relative Proj is quasi-compact as soon as the graded algebra is of finite
  type over its degree-zero part, or generated by its degree-one piece, or has locally
  nilpotent positive part modulo a finite type quasi-coherent degree-zero submodule.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isFiniteType_relativeProj` is **Stacks 084G**: the structure
  morphism of the relative Proj is of finite type as soon as the graded algebra is of finite
  type as a sheaf of algebras over the structure sheaf.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_relativeProjChart` says the chart presentation is inhabited
  for every situation of Stacks 01NN, with no hypothesis on the situation at all, so neither
  of the two lemmas above is vacuous.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isSeparated_relativeProj_of_situation`,
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isQuasiCompact_relativeProj_of_situation` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isFiniteType_relativeProj_of_situation` are the three lemmas above
  with every hypothesis discharged against that witness.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.RelativeProjPoints.homEquivF1`,
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.RelativeProjPoints.pointEquivF1` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.RelativeProjPoints.pointEquivF1_natural` are **Stacks 085D,
  part (1)**: for a graded algebra generated as an `𝒜₀`-algebra by `𝒜₁`, the relative Proj
  represents the functor `F₁` whose value at `T` is the set of isomorphism classes of triples
  `(f : T ⟶ X, ℒ, ψ)` with `ℒ` invertible on `T` and `ψ : f^*𝒜 ⟶ ⨁_{n ≥ 0} ℒ^{⊗ n}` a graded
  algebra map surjective in degree one.  The bijection is natural in the test object.

## Implementation notes

The algebra is a `SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra` over the structure sheaf of the small étale
ringed site of `X`, so its homogeneous pieces are `𝒪_X`-modules in the sense of Stacks 03G9 and
quasi-coherence of each piece is `SheafOfModules.IsQuasicoherentOnSite`, the universe-flexible
form of Stacks 03DL(6).  The universe of the homogeneous pieces is independent of the universe
of the schemes, as it is for `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.Modules`.

A graded algebra in the sense of `SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra` is graded by `ℤ` and is not
assumed commutative.  The graded algebras entering the relative Proj construction are
commutative and concentrated in nonnegative degrees — this is the Stacks project's standing
convention for graded algebras over a base, and it is what the scheme-level
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RelativeProj.GradedAlgebra` of Stacks 01NN builds in — so those two
conditions are fields of the situation.  Commutativity is stated with `HEq` because the two
products of a pair of homogeneous sections of degrees `i` and `j` are sections of
`piece (i + j)` and of `piece (j + i)`, which are different modules.

Stacks 084E, 084F and 084G are stated for a morphism of algebraic spaces carrying a
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.RelativeProjChart`, rather than for a relative Proj built from a
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.RelativeProjSituation`, because the construction of the relative Proj
of an algebraic space — Stacks 084A, 084B, 084C — is not in this library: no declaration here
produces the functor of quadruples or proves it is an algebraic space.  Taking the object
together with its characterizing property as hypotheses, rather than a term, is the same device
`SheafOfModules.GradedAlgebra.IsUniversal` uses for the tensor, exterior and symmetric algebras
of a sheaf of modules, and it is what makes a statement about *the* relative Proj expressible
before one is constructed.  The chart presentation is exactly what Stacks 084B supplies: its
second assertion is a canonical isomorphism `Proj_Z(g^*𝒜) = Z ×_X Proj_X(𝒜)` for a scheme `Z`
over `X`, and the discussion following Stacks 084C applies it to a surjective étale chart.  It
is also exactly what the two proofs of the source use — each reduces to the case of schemes
through that one square — so no content of either lemma is carried by the missing
construction.

The hypotheses of the two lemmas are therefore the scheme-level hypotheses of Stacks 07ZX and
07ZY on the situation living on the chart, which is the restriction `g^*𝒜` of the algebra on
`X`.  That is not a weakening: all three conditions of Stacks 084F, and the condition of
Stacks 084G, are affine-local on the base, so for a quasi-coherent graded algebra on `X` they
hold exactly when they hold for its restriction to a surjective étale chart.  Expressing the
restriction itself would need the algebra on the small étale site of `X` to be transported to a
scheme chart as a sheaf of graded algebras; the single-module half of that transport is
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.restrictToChartScheme`, but no graded version exists, and the
homogeneous pieces of `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.RelativeProjSituation` live in a universe
independent of that of the schemes while those of `Stacks.RelativeProj.Situation` do not, so
the transport also needs the resizing that the source's set-theoretic convention provides.

Stacks 085D is stated for a morphism carrying a `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.RelativeProjPoints`,
for the same reason, and with the scheme-level representing bijections of Stacks 01O4 taken as
a hypothesis: `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjRepresentsF1.F1Representation` is stated in
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/ProjRepresentsF1/Representation.lean` and is nowhere inhabited,
so the existence half of Stacks 01O4 is not available to quote.  Only its three clauses that
are available — the representing bijection at each test scheme, its normalization at the
universal pair, and its naturality — are used, and they are used exactly as the source uses
them: the only property of `𝒜` that enters is the degree-one generation hypothesis, which is
what makes those bijections exist on each chart.

The functor `F₁` is not written as a presheaf, because its functoriality would need the
comparison `g^*(f^*𝒜) = (f ∘ g)^*𝒜` between the restrictions of `𝒜` to two different scheme
points, which is a further clause of Stacks 084B.  Instead the bijection is given over a fixed
point `f` of the base and is natural in the test object above `f`, where the one scheme-level
representation at `f` governs all of them; summing over the points of the base recovers the
bijection `Mor(T, P) = F₁(T)` as
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.RelativeProjPoints.homEquivF1`.  Parts (2) and (3) of Stacks 085D, on
the twists `𝒪_P(n)`, are not stated: they are assertions about sheaves of modules on the
relative Proj itself, which no declaration in this library produces.

